## DESCRIPTION
## Find the equation of a plane passing through three points.  Write the answer in both
## equation and parametric forms.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Inner products)
## DBsection(Applications)
## Date(September 5, 2011)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('lines')

# update 2022.12.12

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",        "PGML.pl",
    "parserVectorUtils.pl", "parserImplicitPlane.pl",
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('ImplicitPlane');
Context()->variables->are(x => 'Real', y => 'Real', z => 'Real');

$pt1 = Point(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));
$pt2 = Point(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));
$pt3 = Point(non_zero_vector3D(-5, 5));

$v1 = ColumnVector($pt2 - $pt1);
$v2 = ColumnVector($pt3 - $pt1);

$v3 = $v1 x $v2;

$ans1 = ImplicitPlane($pt1, $v3);

Context("Vector");
Context()->variables->are(t => 'Real', s => 'Real');
$ans2 = Compute("$pt1 + t $v1 + s $v2");

BEGIN_PGML

The plane [` P `] passes through the points [`[$pt1], [$pt2]`] and [`[$pt3]`].

a) Find [` P `] in the form [` ax + by + cz =d `]: [_____]{$ans1}

b) Find [` P `] in the form [` \vec{P} = \vec{u} + t \vec{v} + s \vec{w}  `]:
    [____]{$ans2}

    Note that you should write this in vector form like [|<1,2,3> + t<4,5,6> + s<7,8,9>|].

END_PGML

($x0, $y0, $z0) = $pt1->value;

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Generally, to find the equation of a plane, one needs it's normal vector.  In
this case, find two vectors parallel to the plane with

    [```\vec{v}_1 = [@ ColumnVector($pt2)@]-[@ ColumnVector($pt1) @] = [$v1], \qquad
    \vec{v}_2 = [@ ColumnVector($pt3) @]-[@ ColumnVector($pt1)@] = [$v2].```]

    Then to find the normal vector, find the cross product:
    [```\vec{n} = \vec{v}_1 \times \vec{v}_2 = [$v3]```]

    Now, the dot product between [`\vec{n}`] and any vector in the plane or
    [`\langle x,y,z\rangle -[$pt1]`] is
    [```[$v3] \cdot \begin{bmatrix} x-[$x0]\\ y-[$y0] \\ z-[$z0] \end{bmatrix} = 0```]
    or simplifying
    [``` [$ans1] ```]

b) Finding this in the form [` \vec{P} = \vec{u} + t \vec{v} + s \vec{w}  `]
the point [`\vec{u}`] is any point in the plane, the vectors [`\vec{v}`] and
[`\vec{w}`] are vectors parallel to the plane.  All of this information is
already found so the answer is
[```[$ans2]```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
